"Chinese medicine traces qi back to two sources, and the distinction matters for understanding why some people seem to start life with more resilience than others, and why daily habits still matter regardless. The first source is what's inherited from a person's parents at conception, sometimes called prenatal qi — a fixed inheritance, stored in the kidney as the source qi discussed in the previous lesson, that can be nourished and protected but not directly topped up once life has begun.": "935598223a81",
"Chinese medicine traces qi back to two sources, and the distinction matters for understanding why some people seem to start life with more resilience than others, and why daily habits still matter regardless. The first source is what's inherited from a person's parents at conception, sometimes called prenatal qi — a fixed inheritance, stored in the kidney as the source qi discussed in the previous lesson, that can be nourished and protected but not directly topped up once life has begun. The second source is what's produced continuously after birth, called postnatal qi, generated from two ongoing inputs: the air taken in through breathing, processed by the lung, and the nutrients taken in through food, processed by the spleen and stomach. Unlike the fixed inheritance from before birth, postnatal qi is renewable — a person with an unremarkable prenatal inheritance who breathes clean air, eats well, and rests adequately can still build a strong overall supply of qi through this second channel. The relationship between the two explains a pattern seen across very different people: someone born with weaker prenatal qi but excellent daily habits can outperform someone born with a stronger inheritance but poor ones, because postnatal qi is doing more of the daily work than the fixed starting amount ever could alone. This is also why this course's lesson on restoring qi focused entirely on daily habits — sleep, food, moderation — rather than on anything a person could do about what they were given at birth.": "7801fc5add1a",
